    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0369" n="357" sameAs="frus:frus1943v02/pg357"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/1620: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-08-28T23:00:00+03:00">August 28, 1943—11
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 29—12:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1216. Personal for the Secretary. Your 749, August 26, 7 p.m., my 1201, August
                    27, 5 p.m. The British Ambassador has received a note from Molotov dated August
                    27 which reads in paraphrased translation as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>[“]The Soviet Government has acquainted itself with the Italian surrender
                            terms which have been approved by the American and British Governments
                            as transmitted by Ambassador Kerr and Ambassador Standley on August
                            26.</p>
                        <p>The Soviet Government approves the terms in question. It empowers General
                            Eisenhower to sign the terms in the negotiations with Castellano on
                            behalf of the Soviet Government.<anchor n="70" xml:id="d322-f1"/>
                        </p>
                        <p>In the present instance it is the opinion of the Soviet Government that a
                            special representative of the Soviet Union is not required when the
                            Italian terms of surrender are signed by General Eisenhower.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d322-f1" n="70" xml:id="d322-f1-content">
                <p>For text of Italian Armistice signed September 3, 1943, but not announced until
                    September 8, see Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic">United States and
                        Italy, 1936–1946</hi>, Documentary Record (Washington, Government Printing
                    Office, 1946), p. 51, or Treaties and Other International Acts Series No. 1604,
                    or 61 Stat. (pt. 3) 2740.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
